	I2CONSET = ( 1 << 5 ); // STA = 1 
	
	Then I2C Peripheral will generate start condition 


I2C Start Condition 
--------------------

	* A high to low condition transition on the SDA line while SCL is high defines a start condition 

		
                  1 | 1
        SCL --------|-----------|---\
                    |           |    \
                    |           |     \
                    |           |      \--------------
                    |           |
                  1 | 1         |
        SDA --------------\     |
                    |      \    |
                    |       \ 0 |
                    |        \--------------------------
                    |           |
                    |           |
                    |           |
                    <-- start -->
                       condition


	I2C start condition is generated automatically by the hardware when the STRAT bit is set ( I2CONSET = (1 << 5)).
	
	This type of programming is called peripheral method, because the I2C peripheral hardware handles signal generation. 

		Types of programming 

			1. Peripheral method 

				* Uses the built-in peripheral 
				* START, STOP, ACK, Clock pulses are generated automatically
				* Programmer only set controls bits & writes data to registers
				* Faster, reliable and commonly used 

			2. Bit banging method 
	
				* signals like (SDA, SCL) are generated manually using GPIO 
				* Programmer toggles pins in software 
				* Slower and CPU intensive 
				* Used when no hardware peripheral is available


I2CONCLR 
--------
		
	   7 	    6        5        4       3       2       1      0
	[ RB3 ] [ I2ENC ] [ STAC ] [ RB2 ] [ SIC ] [ AAC ] [ RB1 ] [ RB2 ] 
	
	
	AAC	:- Assert Acknowledge Clear Bit 
	
	SIC 	:- I2C Intterupt Clear Bit 
	
	STAC 	:- Start Flag Clear Bit

	I2ENC 	:- I2C Interface Disable 

	RBX	:- Reserved Bit 


	I2CONSET & I2CONCLR are internally connected 


Program to generate the start condition 
---------------------------------------

	I2CONSET = (1 << 5); //	STA = 1
	
	I2CONCLR = (1 << 3); // SI Flag Clear 

	while ((( I2CONSET >> 3 ) & 1 ) == 0);

	I2CONCLR = (1 << 5);	// Clear STA flag


I2C Stop Condition 
------------------

A Low to High transition on the SDA line while SCL is high, define a Stop condition. 

	                    1
                      ___|_________________________|__
                     /   |                         |
        SCL         /    |                         |
        ___________/     |                  1      |
                         |               __________|__
                         |              /          |
        SDA              | 0           /           |
        _________________|____________/            |
                         |                         |
                         |                         |
                         |                         |
                         |<---- stop condition --->|



Program to generate STOP condition 
----------------------------------

	I2CONSET = ( 1 << 4 ); // STO = 1

	I2CONCLR = ( 1 << 3 );	// Clear SI flag 

	while ((( I2CONSET >> 3 ) & 1) == 0); // optional 

